00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:56,789
facial gesture switches. So, the job is

550
00:21:56,799 --> 00:21:58,630
to guide the user through that with as

551
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:00,630
little confusion as possible and then

552
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:02,470
make the service reliable once it is

553
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:05,270
enabled. The service also has to survive

554
00:22:05,280 --> 00:22:07,590
normal Android life cycle behavior,

555
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:10,630
device lock and unlock, app updates, and

556
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:13,270
different manufacturer settings. For

557
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:15,510
Switchify PC, there is another setup

558
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:18,149
layer. Pairing over Bluetooth,

559
00:22:18,159 --> 00:22:20,789
confirming the device, keeping the PC

560
00:22:20,799 --> 00:22:22,870
app running in the tray, and installing

561
00:22:22,880 --> 00:22:24,710
it properly. So, Windows allows the

562
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:27,110
right kind of input automation.

563
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:29,270
Those setup details sound boring, but

564
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:31,110
they matter because if setup is too

565
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:33,590
hard, the accessibility feature might as

566
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:35,590
well not exist for the person who needs

567
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:36,870
it.

568
00:22:36,880 --> 00:22:39,590
&gt;&gt; Did AI play a role in creating this at

569
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:42,070
all?

570
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:45,029
Yes, a huge role. I would say AI helped

571
00:22:45,039 --> 00:22:47,510
with about 95% of the implementation

572
00:22:47,520 --> 00:22:50,070
work on recent switchify development.

573
00:22:50,080 --> 00:22:52,070
That does not mean the product idea came

574
00:22:52,080 --> 00:22:55,510
from AI. The need, the design direction,

575
00:22:55,520 --> 00:22:57,430
and the judgment about what is actually

576
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:59,510
usable came from my own experience as a

577
00:22:59,520 --> 00:23:02,630
switch user and AAC user. But AI has

578
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:05,029
been extremely useful for writing code,

579
00:23:05,039 --> 00:23:07,909
refactoring, generating tests, exploring

580
00:23:07,919 --> 00:23:10,310
edge cases, and moving faster through

581
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:11,990
Android and Windows implementation

582
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,630
details. I have also been experimenting

583
00:23:14,640 --> 00:23:17,510
with AI as a testing tool. One example

584
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:19,750
is the codeex and switchify harness I

585
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:22,950
built. It takes a screenshot, asks

586
00:23:22,960 --> 00:23:24,549
codeex to reason about what is on

587
00:23:24,559 --> 00:23:26,710
screen, and then sends a switchify

588
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:29,350
action over ADB as if the agent only had

589
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:31,830
switches. That has been useful because

590
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:34,630
when the AI misunderstands the UX, it

591
00:23:34,640 --> 00:23:36,630
makes me ask whether the UX is actually

592
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:39,669
clear enough. I do not see AI as a

593
00:23:39,679 --> 00:23:41,909
replacement for disabled user testing,

594
00:23:41,919 --> 00:23:43,830
but it can be very powerful when the

595
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:45,350
person directing it understands the

596
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:48,390
access problem deeply.

597
00:23:48,400 --> 00:23:50,470
Let's go into more detail about testing

598
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:53,669
with AI. Can you take us through what AI

599
00:23:53,679 --> 00:23:55,510
is doing here? You mentioned it takes a

600
00:23:55,520 --> 00:23:57,510
screenshot.

601
00:23:57,520 --> 00:23:59,669
The important distinction is that the AI

602
00:23:59,679 --> 00:24:02,470
is not directly controlling Android. I

603
00:24:02,480 --> 00:24:04,070
wrote a parent harness that owns the

604
00:24:04,080 --> 00:24:07,350
emulator, ADB connection, screenshots,

605
00:24:07,360 --> 00:24:09,430
switchify configuration, and every

606
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:12,390
action sent to the device. The AI is

607
00:24:12,400 --> 00:24:13,750
used as the perception and

608
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:16,230
decision-making part of the test. I

609
00:24:16,240 --> 00:24:17,990
start by giving the harness a goal in

610
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,070
ordinary language, such as opening

611
00:24:20,080 --> 00:24:22,310
YouTube or navigating to a particular

612
00:24:22,320 --> 00:24:25,190
control. The harness builds and installs

613
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:27,430
the latest debug version of Switchify on

614
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:30,230
an Android emulator. It checks that the

615
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:32,549
package is installed, the accessibility

616
00:24:32,559 --> 00:24:34,549
service is enabled, and Switchifyy's

617
00:24:34,559 --> 00:24:37,510
private debug bridge is responding. It

618
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:39,110
then backs up the user's existing

619
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:40,870
Switchify settings and configures a

620
00:24:40,880 --> 00:24:43,029
known test profile using manual item

621
00:24:43,039 --> 00:24:45,590
scanning. The test is deliberately

622
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:48,390
limited to an emulator. It does not run

623
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:50,390
against somebody's real phone. And when

624
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:52,310
the test finishes, it restores the

625
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:54,070
Switchify settings and switch mappings

626
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:56,549
it backed up. There is even a separate

627
00:24:56,559 --> 00:24:58,710
cleanup process watching the runner. So

628
00:24:58,720 --> 00:25:00,390
the settings can still be restored if

629
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:03,430
the main test process crashes. Once the

630
00:25:03,440 --> 00:25:05,350
environment is ready, the harness takes

631
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:08,230
a real screenshot using ADB. The

632
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:10,310
screenshot is passed directly to Codeex

633
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:12,789
as an image. So the model is reasoning

634
00:25:12,799 --> 00:25:14,310
from the same visual information

635
00:25:14,320 --> 00:25:17,350
available to a user. I do not give it

636
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:19,350
Android's accessibility tree or let it

637
00:25:19,360 --> 00:25:22,070
inspect the interface with UI automator

638
00:25:22,080 --> 00:25:23,430
partly because that would give it

639
00:25:23,440 --> 00:25:25,830
information a switch user might not have

640
00:25:25,840 --> 00:25:27,750
and partly because Android's automation

641
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:29,830
APIs can interfere with an accessibility

642
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:32,549
service while it is being tested. The

643
00:25:32,559 --> 00:25:34,310
screenshot is supported by a small

644
00:25:34,320 --> 00:25:37,190
amount of diagnostic context. The model

645
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:38,950
is told which application is in the

646
00:25:38,960 --> 00:25:41,110
foreground, whether the Switchify

647
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:43,430
accessibility service is enabled. The

648
00:25:43,440 --> 00:25:45,909
screen dimensions, recent relevant log

649
00:25:45,919 --> 00:25:47,750
messages, and the sequence of switch

650
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:50,549
actions already performed. That action

651
00:25:50,559 --> 00:25:52,310
history matters because a single

652
00:25:52,320 --> 00:25:54,070
screenshot cannot always tell you

653
00:25:54,080 --> 00:25:55,990
whether scanning has just started,

654
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:57,830
whether the highlight has moved or

655
00:25:57,840 --> 00:25:59,510
whether the system has returned to the

656
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:01,909
same state. I also give the model a

657
00:26:01,919 --> 00:26:04,149
technical operating guide for Switchify.

658
00:26:04,159 --> 00:26:05,590
It explains that scanning is

659
00:26:05,600 --> 00:26:07,669
hierarchical. The highlight might

660
00:26:07,679 --> 00:26:09,830
represent a row, a group within that

661
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:12,789
row, or an individual actionable item.

662
00:26:12,799 --> 00:26:14,710
It explains that selecting a row may

663
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:16,390
drill into it rather than activate

664
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:18,390
something immediately. It describes the

665
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:20,710
primary and secondary highlights, the

666
00:26:20,720 --> 00:26:22,870
escape states, keyboard scanning, and

667
00:26:22,880 --> 00:26:25,750
the switchify action menu. Without that

668
00:26:25,760 --> 00:26:27,350
context, the model would naturally

669
00:26:27,360 --> 00:26:28,870
interpret the screen like a touch

670
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:30,390
interface and assume that selecting

671
00:26:30,400 --> 00:26:32,950
something is equivalent to tapping it.

672
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:35,669
For each step, I launch an isolated AI

673
00:26:35,679 --> 00:26:38,470
decision process. It receives the goal,

674
00:26:38,480 --> 00:26:40,390
the current screenshot, the operating

675
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:42,390
guide, the diagnostic state, and the

676
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:45,190
recent action history. It then has to

677
00:26:45,200 --> 00:26:47,110
return a strictly structured JSON

678
00:26:47,120 --> 00:26:49,029
response containing what it observed,

679
00:26:49,039 --> 00:26:51,190
what it thinks should happen next, why

680
00:26:51,200 --> 00:26:53,110
it made that decision, whether the goal

681
00:26:53,120 --> 00:26:55,190
has been achieved, and how confident it

682
00:26:55,200 --> 00:26:58,549
is. In the normal test profile, it can

683
00:26:58,559 --> 00:27:01,110
choose only five responses.

684
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:04,230
next, previous, select, capture, or

685
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:07,990
stop. It cannot invent an ADB command,

686
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,470
tap a coordinate, swipe the screen,

687
00:27:10,480 --> 00:27:12,470
launch an app directly, or use Android

688
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:15,029
back to escape when it gets stuck. Even

689
00:27:15,039 --> 00:27:16,870
if the model returns something outside

690
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:19,269
that list, the parent harness rejects it

691
00:27:19,279 --> 00:27:21,909
before anything reaches the device. When

692
00:27:21,919 --> 00:27:24,310
it chooses an action, the harness sends

693
00:27:24,320 --> 00:27:27,190
an ADB broadcast to Switchifi's debug

694
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:30,310
testing bridge. Switchify converts that

695
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:32,630
into its own internal switch action such

696
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:34,630
as move to the next item or select the

697
00:27:34,640 --> 00:27:37,190
current item. That action goes through

698
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:38,710
the same scanning manager and

699
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:40,549
accessibility service logic used by

700
00:27:40,559 --> 00:27:43,590
Switchify itself. So the AI is not

701
00:27:43,600 --> 00:27:45,990
pretending to press the touch screen. It

702
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:47,669
is interacting through Switchifi's

703
00:27:47,679 --> 00:27:50,310
switch control pathway. The harness then

704
00:27:50,320 --> 00:27:52,310
captures a new screenshot and the cycle

705
00:27:52,320 --> 00:27:55,750
repeats. Observe reason. Choose one

706
00:27:55,760 --> 00:27:58,470
switch action. execute it and observe

707
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:02,230
the result. Each step uses a fresh AI

708
00:28:02,240 --> 00:28:04,470
process, so it cannot rely on an

709
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:06,389
undocumented private memory of what

710
00:28:06,399 --> 00:28:09,269
happened. Any context it needs must come

711
00:28:09,279 --> 00:28:11,430
from the recorded screenshots, state,

712
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:13,110
and action history supplied by the

713
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:15,830
harness. The model can also report

714
00:28:15,840 --> 00:28:17,430
strange behavior as a structured

715
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:20,470
finding. It records what it expected,

716
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:22,789
what actually happened, the visual

717
00:28:22,799 --> 00:28:25,110
evidence, the severity, and even

718
00:28:25,120 --> 00:28:27,350
possible areas of the Android code worth

719
00:28:27,360 --> 00:28:30,230
investigating. The complete run contains

720
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:32,310
every screenshot, decision, action,

721
00:28:32,320 --> 00:28:34,389
warning, and finding, followed by a

722
00:28:34,399 --> 00:28:36,950
final screenshot and a summary that

723
00:28:36,960 --> 00:28:39,190
makes a failed test reproducible instead

724
00:28:39,200 --> 00:28:41,350
of leaving me with only the AI got

725
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:44,070
stuck. What interested me most was where

726
00:28:44,080 --> 00:28:47,029
the AI made the wrong assumption. This

727
00:28:47,039 --> 00:28:48,630
is not about claiming that an AI

728
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:50,789
perfectly represents a disabled user. It

729
00:28:50,799 --> 00:28:52,870
does not. But when it repeatedly

730
00:28:52,880 --> 00:28:54,549
misunderstands whether a highlight is

731
00:28:54,559 --> 00:28:56,870
actionable, whether selecting has moved

732
00:28:56,880 --> 00:28:59,110
down a level, whether a menu is part of

733
00:28:59,120 --> 00:29:01,029
the expected workflow, or whether

734
00:29:01,039 --> 00:29:03,029
anything happened after a switch press,

735
00:29:03,039 --> 00:29:05,909
that gives me pause. The model has the

736
00:29:05,919 --> 00:29:08,389
instructions and the visual evidence.

737
00:29:08,399 --> 00:29:09,750
Yet, the interface is still

738
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:12,389
communicating something ambiguous. That

739
00:29:12,399 --> 00:29:14,549
makes the AI useful as a deliberately

740
00:29:14,559 --> 00:29:17,669
unfamiliar user. Its mistakes can expose

741
00:29:17,679 --> 00:29:19,430
places where I have relied on knowledge

742
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:21,430
that exists in my own head because I

743
00:29:21,440 --> 00:29:23,909
designed the system. The result is not

744
00:29:23,919 --> 00:29:26,070
automatically proof of a user experience

745
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:28,470
problem, but it gives me a precise point

746
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:30,470
to investigate with real switch users

747
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:32,149
rather than assuming the interface is

748
00:29:32,159 --> 00:29:34,149
obvious simply because it is obvious to

749
00:29:34,159 --> 00:29:35,510
me.

750
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:37,430
&gt;&gt; Are you mainly writing prompts or are

751
00:29:37,440 --> 00:29:39,430
there accessibility targeted tools

752
00:29:39,440 --> 00:29:40,470
you're using?

753
00:29:40,480 --> 00:29:43,350
&gt;&gt; Environment is everything. I am using a

754
00:29:43,360 --> 00:29:45,350
generalpurpose AI model rather than a

755
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:47,590
specialized accessibility testing model,

756
00:29:47,600 --> 00:29:49,190
but I have built an accessibility

757
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:51,590
specific environment around it. The

758
00:29:51,600 --> 00:29:53,190
prompt is only one part of that

759
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:55,909
environment. You can write an enormous

760
00:29:55,919 --> 00:29:58,310
prompt explaining switch access, but if

761
00:29:58,320 --> 00:30:00,630
the agent still has access to a mouse,

762
00:30:00,640 --> 00:30:02,549
touchscreen coordinates, or unrestricted

763
00:30:02,559 --> 00:30:05,190
ADB commands, it will naturally take the

764
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:07,669
easiest route. It might complete the

765
00:30:07,679 --> 00:30:09,669
task, but it will not have tested the

766
00:30:09,679 --> 00:30:11,510
experience that a Switch user actually

767
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:14,149
has. A successful result would therefore

768
00:30:14,159 --> 00:30:17,029
tell me very little about Switchify. The

769
00:30:17,039 --> 00:30:18,630
important work was deciding what the

770
00:30:18,640 --> 00:30:20,630
agent could see and what it was allowed

771
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:23,029
to do. It sees a screenshot and a

772
00:30:23,039 --> 00:30:25,029
limited amount of device state. It

773
00:30:25,039 --> 00:30:26,950
receives a Switchify operating guide

774
00:30:26,960 --> 00:30:29,190
explaining scanning, highlight levels,

775
00:30:29,200 --> 00:30:31,669
menus, and escape behavior. Most

776
00:30:31,679 --> 00:30:34,070
importantly, it can normally choose only

777
00:30:34,080 --> 00:30:36,710
next, previous, select, capture, or

778
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:39,110
stop. The parent harness rejects

779
00:30:39,120 --> 00:30:41,830
anything outside those actions. So, I am

780
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:43,590
not merely asking the model to imagine

781
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:45,990
that it uses switches. I have removed

782
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,310
the alternatives. The environment makes

783
00:30:48,320 --> 00:30:50,470
switch access the only available way to

784
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:51,990
progress.

785
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,070
It is the AI equivalent of telling a

786
00:30:54,080 --> 00:30:55,909
developer to put the mouse away for the

787
00:30:55,919 --> 00:30:57,909
day, except the harness can actually

788
00:30:57,919 --> 00:31:00,789
enforce it. There are accessibility

789
00:31:00,799 --> 00:31:02,950
specific components involved, but they

790
00:31:02,960 --> 00:31:04,549
are components I created around the

791
00:31:04,559 --> 00:31:07,669
general AIS, switchifi's debug bridge,

792
00:31:07,679 --> 00:31:09,990
the manual scanning test profile, the

793
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:11,990
action restrictions, the operating

794
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,549
guide, and the evidence system. The

795
00:31:14,559 --> 00:31:17,350
model itself is general purpose. The

796
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:19,110
accessibility knowledge and constraints

797
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:21,350
come from the environment. That

798
00:31:21,360 --> 00:31:23,990
distinction matters beyond this project.

799
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:25,830
Prompting can influence what an agent

800
00:31:25,840 --> 00:31:27,990
says it is doing. But the environment

801
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,549
determines what it can really do. If we

802
00:31:30,559 --> 00:31:32,230
want meaningful accessibility testing

803
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:34,470
from AI, we need accessible test

804
00:31:34,480 --> 00:31:35,990
environments,

805
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:37,830
representative input constraints, and

806
00:31:37,840 --> 00:31:40,230
evidence of the route taken. Otherwise,

807
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:41,669
the agent may reach the right

808
00:31:41,679 --> 00:31:43,590
destination through a pathway the actual

809
00:31:43,600 --> 00:31:45,830
user does not have.

810
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:48,549
&gt;&gt; Are there any accessibility challenges

811
00:31:48,559 --> 00:31:51,110
using AI and agents?

812
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:53,350
Honestly, the biggest accessibility

813
00:31:53,360 --> 00:31:56,070
challenge is very simple. I wish I could

814
00:31:56,080 --> 00:31:58,789
speak to the agent. Working with AI is

815
00:31:58,799 --> 00:32:01,190
supposed to feel conversational. A

816
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:03,269
speaking user can explain an idea,

817
00:32:03,279 --> 00:32:05,509
interrupt when the agent misunderstands,

818
00:32:05,519 --> 00:32:07,430
add a missing detail, and redirect it

819
00:32:07,440 --> 00:32:11,029
almost immediately. As an AAC user,

820
00:32:11,039 --> 00:32:12,710
every one of those interventions takes

821
00:32:12,720 --> 00:32:15,269
more work. I have to compose the

822
00:32:15,279 --> 00:32:17,190
message, navigate the interface, and

823
00:32:17,200 --> 00:32:19,190
make sure the agent has not moved on or

824
00:32:19,200 --> 00:32:20,789
opened something temporary while I'm

825
00:32:20,799 --> 00:32:23,350
still preparing my response. The irony

826
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:25,590
is that the AI can generate and modify

827
00:32:25,600 --> 00:32:27,669
code extremely quickly, but

828
00:32:27,679 --> 00:32:29,430
communicating my intention to it can

829
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:30,870
still be the slowest part of the

830
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:33,350
process. The technology has accelerated

831
00:32:33,360 --> 00:32:35,110
the computer side of the conversation

832
00:32:35,120 --> 00:32:36,870
without necessarily making the human

833
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:39,830
side equally accessible. Most AI

834
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:41,830
products seem to assume one of two

835
00:32:41,840 --> 00:32:44,389
users. somebody typing quickly on a

836
00:32:44,399 --> 00:32:46,470
keyboard or somebody speaking naturally

837
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:49,909
into a microphone. AAC sits awkwardly

838
00:32:49,919 --> 00:32:52,149
between those two models. My

839
00:32:52,159 --> 00:32:54,230
communication is textbased, but that

840
00:32:54,240 --> 00:32:55,750
does not mean it should be treated like

841
00:32:55,760 --> 00:32:58,630
ordinary keyboard input. I may be using

842
00:32:58,640 --> 00:33:01,190
switches, scanning, predictive text, or

843
00:33:01,200 --> 00:33:03,750
generated speech, and each turn can take

844
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:06,070
considerably longer. That is why

845
00:33:06,080 --> 00:33:08,389
timeouts are particularly bad. A

846
00:33:08,399 --> 00:33:10,149
temporary dialogue might be a minor

847
00:33:10,159 --> 00:33:12,549
irritation for somebody using a mouse.

848
00:33:12,559 --> 00:33:15,029
But for a switch or AAC user, it can

849
00:33:15,039 --> 00:33:17,590
make an interaction impossible. By the

850
00:33:17,600 --> 00:33:19,430
time I navigate to it or compose a

851
00:33:19,440 --> 00:33:21,269
response, it may already have

852
00:33:21,279 --> 00:33:23,909
disappeared. If a decision requires my

853
00:33:23,919 --> 00:33:26,389
input, the agent should pause and wait

854
00:33:26,399 --> 00:33:28,389
indefinitely rather than assuming

855
00:33:28,399 --> 00:33:31,509
silence means I am not there. A better

856
00:33:31,519 --> 00:33:33,190
agent interface would understand that

857
00:33:33,200 --> 00:33:34,789
communication speed is not the same

858
00:33:34,799 --> 00:33:36,870
thing as understanding.

859
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:38,630
It would let me pause the agent while I

860
00:33:38,640 --> 00:33:41,269
compose, cue additional instructions,

861
00:33:41,279 --> 00:33:42,950
interrupt using an accessible switch

862
00:33:42,960 --> 00:33:45,350
action, and respond to prompts without a

863
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:47,990
countdown. It would also present its

864
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:49,830
current status in a form that works with

865
00:33:49,840 --> 00:33:52,710
scanning and AAC rather than relying on

866
00:33:52,720 --> 00:33:55,110
rapidly changing visual messages. So,

867
00:33:55,120 --> 00:33:57,590
when I say I wish I could speak, I do

868
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:59,430
not simply mean adding another voice

869
00:33:59,440 --> 00:34:01,750
control button. I mean, I want the same

870
00:34:01,760 --> 00:34:03,590
immediacy in the conversation that

871
00:34:03,600 --> 00:34:06,630
speaking users already have. AI has

872
00:34:06,640 --> 00:34:08,310
enormous potential for disabled

873
00:34:08,320 --> 00:34:10,710
developers, but the interaction model

874
00:34:10,720 --> 00:34:12,310
must recognize different ways of

875
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:14,069
communicating instead of treating speech

876
00:34:14,079 --> 00:34:16,310
and fast typing as the defaults.

877
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:17,909
&gt;&gt; Have you found there's a lot of context

878
00:34:17,919 --> 00:34:19,990
switching when using AI?

879
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:21,750
&gt;&gt; Agents are getting better at maintaining

880
00:34:21,760 --> 00:34:23,430
context and I tend to use massive

881
00:34:23,440 --> 00:34:25,270
threads rather than constantly starting

882
00:34:25,280 --> 00:34:28,149
again. A long thread becomes a kind of

883
00:34:28,159 --> 00:34:31,030
working memory. It contains the original

884
00:34:31,040 --> 00:34:33,909
idea, the decisions we made, corrections

885
00:34:33,919 --> 00:34:35,990
I gave the agent and the reasons behind

886
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:38,629
them. I also plan before asking the

887
00:34:38,639 --> 00:34:40,869
agent to implement something that

888
00:34:40,879 --> 00:34:43,109
reduces context switching because I am

889
00:34:43,119 --> 00:34:44,710
not making every design decision

890
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:47,109
reactively while the agent is already

891
00:34:47,119 --> 00:34:49,990
changing files. I try to establish the

892
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:52,310
goal, constraints, and likely stages

893
00:34:52,320 --> 00:34:54,950
first, then let the agent work through

894
00:34:54,960 --> 00:34:58,069
them. This matters more to me as an AAC

895
00:34:58,079 --> 00:35:00,230
user because changing context is not

896
00:35:00,240 --> 00:35:02,550
just mentally disruptive.

897
00:35:02,560 --> 00:35:04,630
Every correction, repeated explanation,

898
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:06,470
and new instruction has a communication

899
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:09,270
cost. If an agent forgets why a decision

900
00:35:09,280 --> 00:35:11,750
was made, reconstructing that context

901
00:35:11,760 --> 00:35:13,510
may take me considerably longer than it

902
00:35:13,520 --> 00:35:15,589
would take a speaking user. There is

903
00:35:15,599 --> 00:35:17,910
still a limit to massive threads. They

904
00:35:17,920 --> 00:35:20,150
can accumulate outdated assumptions and

905
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:21,829
the agent may give too much weight to

906
00:35:21,839 --> 00:35:24,150
something said much earlier.

907
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:26,790
So planning is important. The thread

908
00:35:26,800 --> 00:35:28,950
needs a clear direction and major

909
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:30,470
decisions should be written down in the

910
00:35:30,480 --> 00:35:32,470
repository or another durable source

911
00:35:32,480 --> 00:35:34,069
rather than existing only in the

912
00:35:34,079 --> 00:35:35,750
conversation.

913
00:35:35,760 --> 00:35:38,230
Overall, context switching is improving

914
00:35:38,240 --> 00:35:40,069
as agents become more capable of

915
00:35:40,079 --> 00:35:42,950
carrying a task from beginning to end.

916
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:44,710
My preference is to give an agent a

917
00:35:44,720 --> 00:35:46,550
substantial piece of work, keep the

918
00:35:46,560 --> 00:35:48,390
context together, and intervene only

919
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:51,109
where my judgment is genuinely needed.

920
00:35:51,119 --> 00:35:53,190
The best agent experience is not one

921
00:35:53,200 --> 00:35:55,910
where I supervise every click. It is one

922
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:58,390
where I establish intent. The agent

923
00:35:58,400 --> 00:36:00,470
maintains that intent across the work

924
00:36:00,480 --> 00:36:02,390
and it returns with evidence of what it

925
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:04,870
actually did.

926
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:06,310
What could be done to make this

927
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:08,630
experience better?

928
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:10,550
&gt;&gt; The biggest improvement for me would be

929
00:36:10,560 --> 00:36:12,310
better control over the agent while it

930
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:15,109
is working. Agents can now do much

931
00:36:15,119 --> 00:36:17,109
larger pieces of work independently,

932
00:36:17,119 --> 00:36:19,589
which is useful. But the controls around

933
00:36:19,599 --> 00:36:21,430
that work still assume somebody can

934
00:36:21,440 --> 00:36:24,069
react quickly with a mouse or keyboard.

935
00:36:24,079 --> 00:36:26,470
I need a reliable way to pause an agent,

936
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:28,870
add information, redirect it, and then

937
00:36:28,880 --> 00:36:30,550
let it continue without losing its

938
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:33,270
progress. Pausing should freeze the task

939
00:36:33,280 --> 00:36:35,670
in a known state. It should not cancel

940
00:36:35,680 --> 00:36:38,230
the work, discard the context, or leave

941
00:36:38,240 --> 00:36:41,510
files half-changed. When I resume, the

942
00:36:41,520 --> 00:36:43,430
agent should briefly confirm what it was

943
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:45,829
doing. Incorporate my new instruction

944
00:36:45,839 --> 00:36:48,069
and continue from that point.

945
00:36:48,079 --> 00:36:51,030
Interrupting is especially important. A

946
00:36:51,040 --> 00:36:52,710
speaking user can often stop someone

947
00:36:52,720 --> 00:36:54,470
mid-sentence as soon as they notice a

948
00:36:54,480 --> 00:36:55,990
misunderstanding.

949
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:58,790
As an AAC user, I may recognize the

950
00:36:58,800 --> 00:37:00,870
problem immediately, but need time to

951
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:03,349
compose the correction. By the time I

952
00:37:03,359 --> 00:37:05,589
send it, the agent may have made several

953
00:37:05,599 --> 00:37:07,190
more decisions based on the original

954
00:37:07,200 --> 00:37:08,950
misunderstanding.

955
00:37:08,960 --> 00:37:10,470
There should be an accessible hold

956
00:37:10,480 --> 00:37:12,950
action that stops new work immediately

957
00:37:12,960 --> 00:37:14,950
while still giving me unlimited time to

958
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:17,589
explain why those controls must be

959
00:37:17,599 --> 00:37:20,550
properly keyboard and switch accessible.

960
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:23,190
Tab and enter are your best friends, but

961
00:37:23,200 --> 00:37:25,270
only if focus order is predictable, and

962
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:28,390
every control can actually be reached.

963
00:37:28,400 --> 00:37:31,430
Pause, stop, resume, and redirect should

964
00:37:31,440 --> 00:37:33,990
have stable positions, clear labels, and

965
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:36,550
configurable switch actions. They should

966
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:38,630
not appear temporarily, move around the

967
00:37:38,640 --> 00:37:41,349
screen, or disappear behind a dialogue.

968
00:37:41,359 --> 00:37:42,950
There also needs to be a distinction

969
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:44,630
between pausing, stopping, and

970
00:37:44,640 --> 00:37:47,030
cancelling. Pausing means keep

971
00:37:47,040 --> 00:37:49,910
everything and wait. Stopping means

972
00:37:49,920 --> 00:37:51,670
finish the current safe operation and

973
00:37:51,680 --> 00:37:54,230
return control to me. Cancelling means

974
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:56,390
abandon the task. And because that can

975
00:37:56,400 --> 00:37:59,109
lose work, it should be deliberate. At

976
00:37:59,119 --> 00:38:01,030
the moment, interfaces sometimes blur

977
00:38:01,040 --> 00:38:02,870
those actions together, which makes

978
00:38:02,880 --> 00:38:05,349
intervention risky. The agent should

979
00:38:05,359 --> 00:38:07,109
also recognize messages arriving while

980
00:38:07,119 --> 00:38:10,310
it is working. If I send a correction,

981
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:11,990
it should not sit ignored until the

982
00:38:12,000 --> 00:38:14,710
entire original task has finished. The

983
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:16,310
agent should reach a safe stopping

984
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:18,310
point, read the new instruction, and

985
00:38:18,320 --> 00:38:19,829
decide whether it changes the current

986
00:38:19,839 --> 00:38:22,790
work. For an AAC user, the message

987
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:24,390
itself should be treated as the

988
00:38:24,400 --> 00:38:26,950
interruption mechanism. I should not

989
00:38:26,960 --> 00:38:28,790
have to race to press a separate button

990
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:31,349
before typing it. This is ultimately

991
00:38:31,359 --> 00:38:34,310
about preserving agency. I want the AI

992
00:38:34,320 --> 00:38:36,710
to work independently, but independence

993
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:37,990
should not mean that it becomes

994
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:40,870
difficult to stop. The ideal system

995
00:38:40,880 --> 00:38:42,550
gives the agent enough freedom to

996
00:38:42,560 --> 00:38:44,550
complete substantial work while ensuring

997
00:38:44,560 --> 00:38:46,390
that I can regain control through one

998
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:48,390
reliable accessible action at any

999
00:38:48,400 --> 00:38:50,950
moment.

1000
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:53,190
&gt;&gt; Is there a better way to train AI tools

1001
00:38:53,200 --> 00:38:57,109
regarding accessibility?

1002
00:38:59,109 --> 00:38:59,119
I think a better approach is to train

1003
00:38:59,119 --> 00:39:01,190
and evaluate AI in constrained

1004
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:03,270
environments where accessibility is not

1005
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:05,589
just described in a prompt but enforced

1006
00:39:05,599 --> 00:39:08,230
by the tools available. If you tell an

1007
00:39:08,240 --> 00:39:10,150
agent to behave like a switch user but

1008
00:39:10,160 --> 00:39:12,150
still give it a mouse touchcreen

1009
00:39:12,160 --> 00:39:14,470
coordinates or unrestricted automation,

1010
00:39:14,480 --> 00:39:16,950
it will usually take the shortest route.

1011
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:19,190
That proves it can complete the task but

1012
00:39:19,200 --> 00:39:20,790
it does not prove that the accessible

1013
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:23,030
route works. The agent needs an

1014
00:39:23,040 --> 00:39:25,109
environment in which next, previous, and

1015
00:39:25,119 --> 00:39:27,910
select are genuinely its only controls.

1016
00:39:27,920 --> 00:39:29,430
That is what I tried to create with the

1017
00:39:29,440 --> 00:39:31,990
Switchify testing harness. The model

1018
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:34,230
sees the screen, receives enough context

1019
00:39:34,240 --> 00:39:35,829
to understand the scanning system, and

1020
00:39:35,839 --> 00:39:38,630
chooses one switch action at a time. The

1021
00:39:38,640 --> 00:39:40,470
surrounding software enforces those

1022
00:39:40,480 --> 00:39:42,790
restrictions. It cannot bypass a

1023
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:44,630
difficult menu by tapping directly on

1024
00:39:44,640 --> 00:39:47,589
the target. If it becomes stuck, that

1025
00:39:47,599 --> 00:39:49,270
difficulty becomes part of the test

1026
00:39:49,280 --> 00:39:51,750
result. This principle can be applied to

1027
00:39:51,760 --> 00:39:54,630
other access methods. An agent testing

1028
00:39:54,640 --> 00:39:56,150
keyboard accessibility should be

1029
00:39:56,160 --> 00:39:58,870
restricted to keyboard input. A screen

1030
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:00,630
reader test agent should primarily

1031
00:40:00,640 --> 00:40:02,390
receive the semantic information and

1032
00:40:02,400 --> 00:40:03,990
announcements available to a screen

1033
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,870
reader user rather than a perfect visual

1034
00:40:06,880 --> 00:40:09,829
representation of the page. Any gaze

1035
00:40:09,839 --> 00:40:11,510
simulation should account for target

1036
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:14,069
size, dwell time, and the difficulty of

1037
00:40:14,079 --> 00:40:16,550
precise selection. The environment

1038
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:18,150
should represent the capabilities and

1039
00:40:18,160 --> 00:40:19,910
limitations of the input method being

1040
00:40:19,920 --> 00:40:22,069
tested. I would still be careful with

1041
00:40:22,079 --> 00:40:25,030
the word training. An AI operating under

1042
00:40:25,040 --> 00:40:26,710
switch constraints does not become a

1043
00:40:26,720 --> 00:40:28,790
switch user and it cannot reproduce

1044
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:31,030
fatigue, involuntary movement,

1045
00:40:31,040 --> 00:40:32,630
communication effort, or somebody's

1046
00:40:32,640 --> 00:40:35,430
lived experience. The aim is not to

1047
00:40:35,440 --> 00:40:38,069
replace testing by disabled people. It

1048
00:40:38,079 --> 00:40:39,910
is to expose basic failures and

1049
00:40:39,920 --> 00:40:42,550
ambiguous interactions earlier before

1050
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:44,230
asking a person to spend their time

1051
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:46,230
finding problems that automated testing

1052
00:40:46,240 --> 00:40:48,630
could have caught. The results should

1053
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:51,270
also be interpreted properly. If an

1054
00:40:51,280 --> 00:40:53,910
agent fails, that does not automatically

1055
00:40:53,920 --> 00:40:56,630
mean the interface is inaccessible.

1056
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:58,390
The model may have misunderstood the

1057
00:40:58,400 --> 00:41:00,710
screen, but that misunderstanding is

1058
00:41:00,720 --> 00:41:03,190
useful evidence. We can inspect the

1059
00:41:03,200 --> 00:41:05,750
screenshots and action history, ask why

1060
00:41:05,760 --> 00:41:08,390
the state was unclear, improve either

1061
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:09,829
the interface or the agents

1062
00:41:09,839 --> 00:41:11,910
accessibility model, and then test

1063
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:14,390
again. So, the better way to teach AI

1064
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:15,910
about accessibility is through

1065
00:41:15,920 --> 00:41:19,030
experience shaped by real constraints.

1066
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:21,349
Documentation explains the rules, but

1067
00:41:21,359 --> 00:41:22,950
the environment determines whether the

1068
00:41:22,960 --> 00:41:25,589
agent must follow them. Accessibility

1069
00:41:25,599 --> 00:41:27,190
should become part of the agents test

1070
00:41:27,200 --> 00:41:29,349
architecture, not a paragraph added to

1071
00:41:29,359 --> 00:41:31,030
the end of its prompt.

1072
00:41:31,040 --> 00:41:32,870
How did you create Switchify without

1073
00:41:32,880 --> 00:41:35,270
having it in the first place? For

1074
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:37,670
example, um how do you program or

1075
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:40,470
control your ID, etc. And what's that

1076
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:43,829
like as a user? I had been using iOS for

1077
00:41:43,839 --> 00:41:46,470
about 10 years. And for desktop access,

1078
00:41:46,480 --> 00:41:48,630
I was using grid 3 right up until a few

1079
00:41:48,640 --> 00:41:51,670
days ago when I released Switchify PC.

1080
00:41:51,680 --> 00:41:53,670
So, I used the access tools I already

1081
00:41:53,680 --> 00:41:55,990
had, mainly grid 3, to control the

1082
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:58,870
computer and build the first versions.

1083
00:41:58,880 --> 00:42:01,829
The answer is slowly and with a lot of

1084
00:42:01,839 --> 00:42:04,790
patience. I was using existing assistive

1085
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:06,390
technology to build a new assist of

1086
00:42:06,400 --> 00:42:09,430
technology tool. In a way, that also

1087
00:42:09,440 --> 00:42:11,910
showed why Switchify needed to exist

1088
00:42:11,920 --> 00:42:13,670
because the process made the barriers

1089
00:42:13,680 --> 00:42:16,309
very obvious.

1090
00:42:16,319 --> 00:42:18,390
&gt;&gt; Are there any differences between iOS,

1091
00:42:18,400 --> 00:42:19,829
Android, or Windows you've had to

1092
00:42:19,839 --> 00:42:21,109
handle?

1093
00:42:21,119 --> 00:42:24,230
&gt;&gt; Yes. Each platform exposes accessibility

1094
00:42:24,240 --> 00:42:26,710
and input APIs differently, but one of

1095
00:42:26,720 --> 00:42:28,550
the biggest differences is that touch is

1096
00:42:28,560 --> 00:42:31,270
fundamentally different from a mouse. On

1097
00:42:31,280 --> 00:42:33,589
Android and iOS, you are often trying to

1098
00:42:33,599 --> 00:42:35,510
simulate direct interaction with a touch

1099
00:42:35,520 --> 00:42:39,030
screen. Taps, swipes, long presses,

1100
00:42:39,040 --> 00:42:40,950
multi-touch gestures, and screen

1101
00:42:40,960 --> 00:42:43,829
positions. On Windows, control is built

1102
00:42:43,839 --> 00:42:45,750
around a pointer, clicks, keyboard

1103
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:48,550
input, windows, focus, hover states,

1104
00:42:48,560 --> 00:42:50,790
drag operations, and desktop UI

1105
00:42:50,800 --> 00:42:53,510
conventions that changes the design of

1106
00:42:53,520 --> 00:42:55,990
the access system. A touch gesture is

1107
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:57,990
usually a bounded action at a point or

1108
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:00,470
along a path. A mouse interaction can

1109
00:43:00,480 --> 00:43:02,550
involve moving through empty space,

1110
00:43:02,560 --> 00:43:04,710
hovering before clicking, dragging

1111
00:43:04,720 --> 00:43:07,190
between windows, using the wheel, or

1112
00:43:07,200 --> 00:43:08,950
relying on focus in a way that mobile

1113
00:43:08,960 --> 00:43:11,910
apps often do not. iOS has strong

1114
00:43:11,920 --> 00:43:13,670
built-in accessibility features and

1115
00:43:13,680 --> 00:43:16,150
consistency, but third party developers

1116
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:18,069
have less freedom to build deep access

1117
00:43:18,079 --> 00:43:20,950
tools. Android gives more flexibility

1118
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:22,550
through accessibility services and

1119
00:43:22,560 --> 00:43:24,390
gesture dispatch, but device

1120
00:43:24,400 --> 00:43:26,790
differences, OEM behavior, and the

1121
00:43:26,800 --> 00:43:28,630
quality of app accessibility trees can

1122
00:43:28,640 --> 00:43:31,349
be challenging. Windows gives a lot of

1123
00:43:31,359 --> 00:43:33,910
power for desktop control, but the range

1124
00:43:33,920 --> 00:43:35,990
of apps and UI technologies is much

1125
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:38,069
wider, so consistency becomes the hard

1126
00:43:38,079 --> 00:43:39,990
problem.

1127
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:43,589
What can each OS learn from one another?

1128
00:43:43,599 --> 00:43:46,150
&gt;&gt; Android can learn from iOS's consistency

1129
00:43:46,160 --> 00:43:49,349
and polish and built-in accessibility.

1130
00:43:49,359 --> 00:43:51,510
One of the strengths of iOS is that the

1131
00:43:51,520 --> 00:43:53,349
system accessibility features feel

1132
00:43:53,359 --> 00:43:55,589
integrated and predictable across a lot

1133
00:43:55,599 --> 00:43:58,790
of apps. iOS can learn from Android's

1134
00:43:58,800 --> 00:44:00,470
openness and willingness to let

1135
00:44:00,480 --> 00:44:03,750
developers build deeper access tools.

1136
00:44:03,760 --> 00:44:05,829
Android accessibility services give

1137
00:44:05,839 --> 00:44:07,910
developers more room to experiment with

1138
00:44:07,920 --> 00:44:10,630
things like scanning, gesture dispatch,

1139
00:44:10,640 --> 00:44:13,349
and alternative input. And that openness

1140
00:44:13,359 --> 00:44:15,670
is important because not every user fits

1141
00:44:15,680 --> 00:44:18,309
the built-in model. Windows can learn

1142
00:44:18,319 --> 00:44:20,309
from mobile platforms about simpler,

1143
00:44:20,319 --> 00:44:21,829
